This Stainless Runtime Library Exception ("Exception") is an additional
permission under section 7 of the GNU General Public License, version 3
("GPLv3"). It applies to a given file (the "Runtime Library") that bears a
notice placed by the copyright holder of the file stating that the file is
governed by GPLv3 along with this Exception.

When you use Stainless to compile a program, Stainless combines portions of
its runtime library and standard library with the compiled program. The
purpose of this Exception is to allow compilation of non-GPL (including
proprietary) programs to use, in this way, the files covered by this
Exception.


  ON THE RELATIONSHIP TO THE GCC RUNTIME LIBRARY EXCEPTION

  This document is modelled closely on the GNU GCC Runtime Library
  Exception, version 3.1, published by the Free Software Foundation:

      https://www.gnu.org/licenses/gcc-exception-3.1.html

  It is not that document, and is not published by or endorsed by the
  FSF. The GCC exception could not be used directly here: its grant is
  conditional on an "Eligible Compilation Process", which it defines in
  terms of GCC specifically, and it may not be modified. What follows is
  therefore a grant made by the copyright holder of Stainless, following
  the same structure and using the same terms of art so that its effect
  is easy to compare against the original.

  This wording has not been reviewed by a lawyer. If the licensing of
  this project matters commercially to you, satisfy yourself of its
  effect before relying on it.


0. Definitions.

A file is an "Independent Module" if it either requires the Runtime Library
for execution after a Compilation Process, or makes use of an interface
provided by the Runtime Library, but is not otherwise based on the Runtime
Library.

"Stainless" means a version of the Stainless compiler and its accompanying
tooling, with or without modifications, governed by version 3 (or a
specified later version) of the GNU General Public License with the option
of using any subsequent versions published by the FSF.

"GPL-compatible Software" is software whose conditions of propagation,
modification and use would permit combination with Stainless in accord with
the license of Stainless.

"Target Code" refers to output from any compiler for a real or virtual
target processor architecture, in executable form or suitable for input to
an assembler, loader, linker and/or execution phase. Notwithstanding that,
Target Code does not include data in any format that is used as a compiler
intermediate representation, or used for producing a compiler intermediate
representation. In particular, the LLVM intermediate representation that
Stainless emits, whether as text or in any other form, is not Target Code.

The "Compilation Process" transforms code entirely represented in
non-intermediate languages designed for human-written code into Target
Code. Thus, for example, use of source code generators and preprocessors
need not be considered part of the Compilation Process, since the
Compilation Process can be understood as starting with the output of the
generators or preprocessors.

A Compilation Process is "Eligible" if it is done using Stainless, alone or
with other GPL-compatible software, or if it is done without using any work
based on Stainless. For example, using non-GPL-compatible software to
optimize any Stainless or LLVM intermediate representations would not
qualify as an Eligible Compilation Process.


1. Grant of Additional Permission.

You have permission to propagate a work of Target Code formed by combining
the Runtime Library with Independent Modules, even if such propagation would
otherwise violate the terms of GPLv3, provided that all Target Code was
generated by Eligible Compilation Processes. You may then convey such a
combination under terms of your choice, consistent with the licensing of the
Independent Modules.


2. No Weakening of Stainless Copyleft.

The availability of this Exception does not imply any general presumption
that third-party software is unaffected by the copyleft requirements of the
license of Stainless.


3. In practice.

This section is a summary and grants nothing beyond the above.

Compiling your own program with Stainless does not place that program under
the GNU General Public License. You may license what you write however you
wish, and distribute it in binary form, even though the Stainless runtime
and standard library are compiled into it.

Modifying Stainless itself, or modifying the Runtime Library, and
distributing the result to others does carry the full obligations of the
GNU General Public License, version 3.
